course: dictionary
lesson: 3963
title: Linking your ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Good writers do not just list facts. They join their ideas together so the reader can follow the argument. «Furthermore» is a linking word, and it means in addition: it tells the reader that another point is coming, and that it adds to the one before. In «It's raining. Furthermore, it's freezing», the second point makes the bad day even worse.
explain: Notice the comma after «furthermore». It usually starts a new sentence that gives an extra or stronger point, so it works well when you want to build your case, one step at a time.
explain: In everyday talk people usually say «also», «and» or «what's more». You will meet «furthermore» mostly in writing, especially in speeches and arguments, where it makes your points sound strong and well organised.
explain: «Chatbots have a typical style, with words like "furthermore" and "in conclusion", that teachers learn to notice.» A string of linking words can be a clue to how a text was written, but a skilled writer uses them only when they really help the reader.
fact: Furthermore means in addition, used to add another point.
fact: «Furthermore» is a linking word, also called a connective.
fact: It is often used in persuasive and discussion writing, in an order like «Firstly…», «Furthermore…», «In conclusion…».
fact: It often adds a stronger point, as in «It's raining. Furthermore, it's freezing».
fact: In speech people more often say «also», «and» or «what's more».
exercise: What does «furthermore» mean? | Because of that | Earlier, before this | *In addition, adding another point | Instead, the opposite
exercise: Mia writes: «Pets are good for us. ___, they teach children to be kind.» Which word fits the gap? | Quietly | Under | *Furthermore | Yesterday
exercise: Ben starts a sentence with «Furthermore». What is he doing? | *Adding another point to his argument | Asking a question | Ending his story | Saying sorry
test: «The bus was late, and furthermore, it was full.» What does «furthermore» add here? | The end of the story | *Another problem on top of the first one | A sorry for being late | A question about the bus route
test: Which word could replace «furthermore» in «It's raining. Furthermore, it's freezing»? | Quietly | *What's more | Under | Yesterday
exam: In a persuasive writing task, Sam wants his last point to sound strong. Which linking word should he use? | Under the table | Furthermore | *In conclusion, or furthermore for another strong point | Once upon a time
